Given these two classes, which property is the one the database actually stores the ticket-to-comment relationship in?

```csharp
public class Ticket
{
    public long Id { get; set; }
    public List<Comment> Comments { get; set; } = new();
}

public class Comment
{
    public long Id { get; set; }
    public long TicketId { get; set; }
    public Ticket? Ticket { get; set; }
}
```

Larkspur's rule is: **every comment belongs to a ticket**. Here is the model as written.

```csharp
public class Comment
{
    public long Id { get; set; }
    public string Body { get; set; } = "";
    public long? TicketId { get; set; }
    public Ticket? Ticket { get; set; }
}

modelBuilder.Entity<Comment>()
    .HasOne(c => c.Ticket)
    .WithMany(t => t.Comments)
    .HasForeignKey(c => c.TicketId);
```

The relationship is configured and the foreign key is on the right end. Which change is required for the database to actually enforce the rule?

solution.code:
function foreignKeyOwner(relationship) {
  const kind = relationship.kind;
  if (kind === 'many-to-many') return 'join-table';
  if (kind === 'one-to-many') return relationship.right;
  if (kind === 'many-to-one') return relationship.left;
  if (kind === 'one-to-one') {
    return relationship.principal === relationship.left ? relationship.right : relationship.left;
  }
  return 'unknown';
}

grading.starterCode:
function foreignKeyOwner(relationship) {
  // The dependent end holds the foreign key. Which end is that?
  return relationship.left;
}

items.workedExampleMarkdown:
You add a column:

```csharp
public class Ticket
{
    public long Id { get; set; }
    public string Subject { get; set; } = "";
    public string Priority { get; set; } = "normal";   // new
}
```

Then:

```bash
dotnet ef migrations add AddTicketPriority
dotnet ef database update
```

The first command does **not** look at your database. It compares your current model against a snapshot of the model as of the last migration — a checked-in file — and writes the difference as code:

```csharp
public partial class AddTicketPriority : Migration
{
    protected override void Up(MigrationBuilder migrationBuilder)
    {
        migrationBuilder.AddColumn<string>(
            name: "Priority", table: "Tickets",
            nullable: false, defaultValue: "normal");
    }

    protected override void Down(MigrationBuilder migrationBuilder)
    {
        migrationBuilder.DropColumn(name: "Priority", table: "Tickets");
    }
}
```

Three things follow from 'it compares against a snapshot, not the database'.

**The migration is a reviewable artefact.** It is C# in your repository. Read it before applying it, the same way you would read hand-written SQL. `dotnet ef migrations script` prints the SQL if you would rather review that.

**`defaultValue` matters for existing rows.** The column is `NOT NULL`; every row already in the table needs a value at the moment the column appears. Without the default, applying this to a populated database fails — which is far better than the alternative of finding out in production, and is exactly why you apply migrations to a copy first.

**Drift is possible.** If someone changes the database by hand, the snapshot no longer describes reality, and the next migration will be computed from a fiction. The database is not the source of truth; the migration history is.

The deployment ordering question is the same one from chapter 12, unchanged by the language. A migration that adds something is safe to apply *before* the new code ships, because old code ignores a column it does not know about. A migration that removes or renames something is only safe *after* every running instance has stopped referring to it. That is why a rename is usually three deployments — add the new, write both, drop the old — rather than one clever step.

